through a long lens.

172
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:29,000
And we're going to look at these through
the rest of the video now.

173
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:44,000
But first and very quickly, you have to
understand the valuation backdrop, which
is the ideal setup in addition to earnings
growth long profiles that we look for.

174
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:52,000
So if you think about price earnings
ratios and you think of the first period
and the second period.

175
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:59,000
So the first period being the current year
and the second period being next year.

176
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:17,000
So we have PE1 and we have PE2 and PE1 is
simply the current stock price of the
stock divided by the forward estimate of
EPS for the current year.

177
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:27,000
And PE2 is the current stock price divided
by forward looking EPS for the following
year, next year.

178
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:37,000
So the ideal setup that we want to see
from a stock is obviously to see EPS
growing.

179
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:55,000
So in the second year, if we divide the
current stock price with EPS of the second
year, we're going to get a lower PE ratio
than the first year because we're dividing
by a higher number.

180
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:10,000
So the EPS is higher in period 2,
therefore using the same stock price for
period 1 and period 2, the PE ratio in
period 2 is going to be lower than period
1.

181
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:24,000
Additionally, what we want to see is in
both periods, the PE ratio for the stock
is higher than the PE ratio for the
sector.

182
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:30,000
Now, how do we work out the sector PE
ratio?

183
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:37,000
Well, we need a mean average EPS estimate
for the sector.

184
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:50,000
And we do this by adding up all of the EPS
numbers in each period, PE1 and PE2, and
then dividing by the amount of stocks in
the sector.

185
00:29:50,000 --> 00:30:15,000
When we see in both periods that we have a
higher PE premium to the sector from the
stock, it means we have confirmation of
the valuation that the market is pricing a
premium of the stock in valuation terms
versus the sector.

186
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:28,000
This gives us evidence that the market
likes the stock and is prepared to pay for
the growth and pay a premium to the
sector.

187
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:49,000
What you will also notice is that the PE
ratio in period 2 for the sector is lower
than in period 1, which means the average
earnings per share of the sector is
increasing from period 1 to period 2.

188
00:30:49,000 --> 00:31:02,000
Earnings per share is increasing for the
stock as well over the 2 periods, but the
stock trades on a premium to the sector
for both periods.

189
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:15,000
So, this is the ideal valuation backdrop
when we're processing, looking for long
ideas when we're doing our quant
processing.

190
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:33,000
So, we have a stock in the sector that is
trading in period 1 and period 2 on a
premium to the sector, and it has higher
EPS in period 2 versus period 1.

191
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:58,000
Now, in tandem with this, the ideal
situation when we're doing our quant
processing and looking for long ideas, the
ideal setup is not only to have a PE
premium on the stock versus the sector in
period 1 and period 2,

192
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:07,000
where we have EPS going higher, what we
also want is the ideal earnings growth
setup.

193
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:21,000
So, we have our two periods, earnings
growth 1, which is the percentage increase
in EPS from period 0 to period 1.

194
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:32,000
So, EG1 percentage, the percentage
increase in EPS from last year to current
year.

195
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:48,000
So, EG0, last year's EPS that is reported,
and the percentage increase to period 1,
which is an EPS forward-looking estimate,
and the percentage increase from 0, last
year to period 1.

196
00:32:48,000 --> 00:33:13,000
And what we want to see from EG1
percentage is that the company's earnings
per share is growing at a faster rate than
the sector earnings growth percentage
increase rate.

197
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:28,000
So, you'll also notice here as well that
the sector is growing positively, so the
earnings growth with the sector average is
actually growing as well.

198
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:44,000
So, in an ideal backdrop, what we want to
see is that the sector earnings growth is
growing faster in EG2 versus EG1.

199
00:33:44,000 --> 00:34:01,000
And then when you have a look at the stock
itself, you want to see in earnings growth
2 that the rate of growth for the stock is
faster in earnings growth 2 versus
earnings growth 1.

200
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:08,000
But it's also higher than the sector rate
of growth in EG2 period.

201
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:24,000
So, the earnings growth for the stock is
faster than the sector in both periods,
but for the stock itself, it is faster in
the second period versus the first period.

202
00:34:24,000 --> 00:34:41,000
And what we call this, when we just look
at the stock itself, so in absolute terms,
we call it sequentially positive earnings
growth momentum.

203
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:54,000
And when we look at it in comparison
relative to the sector, we call it
sequentially positive above sector average
earnings growth momentum.

204
00:34:54,000 --> 00:35:07,000
The easiest way to think of this is that
the stock, the company itself, its
earnings are growing faster than the
sector in both periods.

205
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:18,000
And in period 2, the rate of increase for
the company itself is faster in period 2
versus period 1.

206
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:21,000
So, we have earnings growth momentum.

207
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:41,000
So, this is the ideal scenario that we are
looking for for our earnings growth
profile for long trade ideas with the
backdrop of the ideal setup for valuation
for price earnings ratio.

208
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:53,000
Now, there are obviously other earnings
growth profiles that are available to all
traders and indeed all investors.

